module Darklang.Cli.Docs.ForAI

let content(): String =
  """# Darklang AI Quick Reference

"""
  + SyntaxAtAGlance.content()
  + """
Darklang is a persistent package tree, not a directory of scripts. CLI process
state resets between calls; package changes persist.

## A command-line tool

Save a module, run its `main` by name, and test it:

  module /Me.Greet - <<'EOF'
  let greeting(name: String): String = "Hello, " + name
  let main(args: List<String>): Int64 =
    match args with
    | [ name ] ->
      Stdlib.printLine(greeting(name))
      0
    | _ ->
      Stdlib.printErrorLine("usage: Me.Greet.main <name>")
      2
  EOF
  run Me.Greet.main Ada    # `args` is ["Ada"]; exits 0
  module /Me.Greet.Tests - <<'EOF'
  test hello = Me.Greet.greeting("Ada") |> Stdlib.Test.equal("Hello, Ada")
  EOF
  test run Me.Greet

Save the module again to change it.

## Workflow

  search --batch "List.map" "Json.serialize" --fn  # three results per query
  docs signatures Stdlib.String Stdlib.List        # every fn in these modules
  view <name>                                      # one item's source
  eval 'Stdlib.List.length([1, 2])'                # one expression
  status                                           # what changed
  commit "message" -y                              # record it
  run app.dark [args]                              # a file instead; `docs scripts`

`search` takes `--fn`, `--type`, `--val` or `--test` to narrow, and `--with-docs`
for full comments. To find a function by what it takes and returns, search the
types: `search bytes to string` or `search String->Int64`.

Answers go on stdout; errors, hints and logs go on stderr; a refusal exits
non-zero. Under `--json` a refusal leaves stdout empty, so test the exit code.

## Functions most programs need

  Stdlib.Int64.parse(s)   Stdlib.Float.parse(s)   # Result<_, ParseError>
  Stdlib.Int64.toFloat(n)   Stdlib.Float.round(x)  # round gives an Int64
  Stdlib.String.split(s, ",")   Stdlib.String.join(xs, ",")   Stdlib.String.trim(s)
  Stdlib.String.toList(s)   Stdlib.String.padStart(s, '0', 2)
  Stdlib.List.range(1, n)   Stdlib.List.getAt(xs, i)   Stdlib.List.contains(xs, x)
  Stdlib.Int64.random(1, 6)   Stdlib.List.randomElement(xs)   # both ends included
  Stdlib.Cli.Stdin.readLineOrEnd()   # Option<String>, None at the end of input
  Stdlib.Cli.Posix.sleep(1000.0)   Stdlib.DateTime.now()            # sleep takes ms

## Files, HTTP and JSON

  Stdlib.Cli.FileSystem.readFileAsString(path)       # Result<String, FileError>
  Stdlib.Cli.FileSystem.writeFile(path, Stdlib.Utf8.encode(text))
  Stdlib.String.splitOnNewline(text)
  Stdlib.HttpClient.get(url, [("user-agent", "me")]) # r.statusCode, r.body (Bytes)
  Stdlib.Utf8.decode(r.body)                         # Result<String, String>
  Stdlib.Json.parse<Profile>(text)                   # Result<Profile, ParseError>
  Stdlib.Json.serialize<Profile>(value)

A JSON object parses into a record: extra fields are ignored, and a field that
may be absent or `null` is an `Option` field.

Files and the network are denied until allowed. When Dark denies access, submit
the exact `permissions allow` command from the error through your runner's
approval flow. Run it separately from the task, without asking in chat first.
Ask me requests your approval; Approve for me uses the runner's reviewer;
bypass/dangerous mode skips review.

In Codex, request escalation even when the policy path is writable. In Claude,
use normal Bash review without allow or ask rules for permission commands.

Grants persist for all code in the instance. If review is denied or unavailable,
stop; do not bypass it. After the command succeeds, retry only if doing so will
not repeat earlier side effects. This guidance relies on runner review; it
does not prevent direct policy edits.

To configure a whole project manually:

  permissions profile local-dev /abs/path/to/project --yes

One host, every path on it: `permissions allow http GET https://api.example.com`.

## Mistakes that cost a run

- There is no `Stdlib.Int64.toString`. `$"{x}"` works for any value: it uses the
  type's `Display` impl (Strings, numbers, Bool, Char, DateTime, Uuid, Option,
  Result, ...; `impls Display`) and falls back to `Stdlib.inspect(x)`.
  `Stdlib.display(x)` needs a `Display` impl; `Stdlib.inspect(x)` shows any value.
  An operator is not a function either: `a % b`, not `Stdlib.Int64.mod(a, b)`.
- Operands must have one type: `1 + 2.0` is an error.
- No partial application: write the lambda, `fun x -> Stdlib.max(1, x)`.
- `|>` passes the left value as the first argument. An `if` or `match` on its
  left needs parentheses.
- Every line of a block but the last returns Unit: `let _ = f(x)` drops a value.
- Lambdas: `fun x ->`, `fun(acc, x) ->`; one tuple parameter is `fun((a, b)) ->`.
- A tuple inside a constructor keeps its own parentheses: `Some((a, b))`.
  Tuple parts are `pair.1`, `pair.2`, or `let (a, b) = pair`.
- Records: `State { count = 0 }` builds, `{ state with count = 1 }` updates.
- Chars use single quotes, `'a'`. Interpolation is `$"x = {x}"`.
- `f(x)?` returns early from the function on `Error` or `None`.
- Modules are not values: `let c = Stdlib.List` is invalid.
- A nested function annotates its parameters and cannot call one defined below it.
- A keyword such as `type` is a name only in double backticks: ``type``.
- `Stdlib.X` is `Darklang.Stdlib.X`. Your own names go under an owner such as
  `Me`; `Darklang` is reserved.

Logs: ~/.darklang/logs/cli.log

## More
  docs for-ai packages      saving and changing declarations, the type checker, a command-line tool
  docs for-ai tests         package tests in full
  docs for-ai permissions   rules, profiles and package approvals
  docs for-ai traces        what a run did, without print statements
  docs for-ai scm           drafts, commits, branches, deprecation
  docs for-ai syntax        the rest of the syntax notes, and name resolution
  docs for-ai tests http-server   several parts and topics in one call
  docs scripts | syntax | errors | stdlib | signatures <module>
  help <command>"""
